---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import ProcessFaqAccordion from '@/components/ProcessFaqAccordion';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title =
  'How Chinese Parents Can Fund an EB-5 Investment for a Child in the U.S. | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'A guide for Chinese parents considering funding an EB-5 investment for a child studying in the U.S. — covering gifts, source of funds, and documentation before moving capital.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-chinese-families`;
const h1 = 'How Chinese Parents Can Fund an EB-5 Investment for a Child Studying in the U.S.';
const subhead =
  'What families need to document before moving capital toward a child\'s EB-5 investment — and why the paper trail matters as much as the funds.';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'Ways to Fund EB-5 — Gifted, Loaned, and Crypto Paths',
    url: '/research/ways-to-fund-eb5-investment-2026',
    description: 'Gift, loan, and other lawful funding paths USCIS reviews for EB-5 capital.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Pre-Investment Checklist',
    url: '/research/investment/eb5-pre-investment-checklist-part-1-f1-h1b',
    description: 'What to verify before committing capital and filing I-526E.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EB-5 for Chinese International Students',
    url: '/pathways/eb5-for-chinese-students',
    description: 'Student-side decision framing for Chinese F-1 applicants evaluating EB-5.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Does the student have to prove they earned the investment money themselves?',
    answer:
      'No. Gifted funds are commonly used in EB-5 cases. What matters is that the original source of the funds — usually the parent\'s — is lawful and well documented.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How far back does source of funds documentation need to go?',
    answer:
      'This depends on the specific funding history and case, and current USCIS practice can change. An immigration attorney experienced in EB-5 source-of-funds cases should review the specific facts before a family finalizes a funding plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What if funds come from a business the parent owns in China?',
    answer:
      'Business income can be used as a source of funds, but it typically requires more extensive documentation — business licenses, tax filings, audited financials, and a clear link between the business income and the gifted funds. This is an area where working with an adviser familiar with both Chinese business documentation and USCIS expectations matters most.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone Who Understands Cross-Border EB-5 Funding"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for Chinese Families"
  keywords={[
    'Chinese parents EB-5 funding',
    'EB-5 gift funds China',
    'EB-5 source of funds parents',
    'fund EB-5 for child in US',
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="extraCompliance">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed border border-border rounded-lg bg-secondary/40 p-4 m-0">
      This page provides educational information only and does not constitute legal, financial, tax, or immigration
      advice. Consult a qualified immigration attorney and a cross-border tax adviser before moving investment capital
      or entering into a gift arrangement.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2>Who this page is for</h2>
  <p>
    In many EB-5 cases involving Chinese international students, the child is the investor of record, but a parent or
    other family member is the actual source of the funds. This is a normal and accepted structure in EB-5 — USCIS does
    not require the investor to have personally earned the investment capital. What USCIS does require is a clear,
    documented, lawful trail showing where the money came from and how it reached the investor. This page focuses on
    that funding and documentation process; for the broader student decision (EB-5 vs. the H-1B path), see our guide to
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-chinese-students">EB-5 for Chinese international students</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How the gift structure typically works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Establish the gift relationship.</strong> A parent (or other family member) formally gifts funds to the
      student investor, documented with a signed gift letter or deed of gift.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Document the parent's source of funds.</strong> USCIS looks through to the original source of the gifted
      funds, not just the gift itself. This can include income records, business ownership documentation, property sale
      records, or other lawful sources going back multiple years.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trace the path of funds.</strong> Bank records showing the money moving from the parent's account to the
      student's account, and then to the new commercial enterprise or regional center escrow account, need to form a
      complete, unbroken paper trail.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Address currency movement.</strong> Moving significant capital out of China involves its own set of
      considerations under Chinese foreign exchange rules. Families typically work with both a China-side financial/legal
      adviser and their U.S. immigration attorney to plan this properly and in advance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prepare documentation before, not after.</strong> Gathering these records early — ideally before a project
      is selected or a petition is filed — gives families more time to address any gaps.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common documentation questions</h2>
  <div class="not-prose mb-8">
    <ProcessFaqAccordion client:load items={faqItems} />
  </div>

  <h2>Tax and advisory considerations</h2>
  <p>
    Moving investment capital across borders can carry tax implications on both sides — U.S. and China — separate from
    the immigration process itself. This page does not provide tax or legal advice. Families considering this path
    typically work with a cross-border tax adviser and a U.S. immigration attorney together, early in the process,
    rather than addressing tax questions after funds have already moved.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
